{"qid":"Q105313","names":["Kingdom of Wessex"],"title":"Kingdom of Wessex","endonym":{"native":"Ƿestseaxna rīċe","lang":"ang"},"capsule":"Wessex (Ƿestseaxna rīċe — 'Kingdom of the West Saxons') was founded traditionally in 519 AD by Cerdic and Cynric in the upper Thames and Hampshire region, and grew through the sixth and seventh centuries to dominate southern England. Under Alfred the Great (r. 871–899), Wessex repelled the Great Heathen Army of the Danes, partitioned England with the Danelaw at the Treaty of Alfred and Guthrum (c. 886), and laid the administrative and intellectual foundations of a unified English state. Alfred's successors — Edward the Elder, Athelstan, and Edmund — completed the reconquest of the Danelaw; Athelstan became the first king formally recognized as 'King of the English' after his victory at the Battle of Brunanburh in 937. The distinct Kingdom of Wessex merged into the unified Kingdom of England under Athelstan's successors, with 927 or 936 as the conventional end dates.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Core Wessex territory in southern England is reasonably attested in Anglo-Saxon chronicles, but precise year-by-year boundaries — especially vis-à-vis Mercia, the Danelaw, and Cornwall — are uncertain; the attested window 534–936 reflects scholarly estimates for the polity's documented existence.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Wessex","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Wessex"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Wessex","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wessex"}],"britannica":"place/Wessex-historical-kingdom","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Wessex","rulers":[{"qid":n